---
import CaseStudy from "../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import Figure from "../../components/Figure.astro";

const metrics = [
  { value: "$1.6M", label: "Annual fare leakage, upper bound" },
  { value: "78.2%", label: "Defects the cheaper control rule misses" },
  { value: "0.7363", label: "Adjusted R-squared, selected model" },
  { value: "6", label: "Business problems, one per method" },
];
---

<CaseStudy
  title="The regression did not support the complaint it was built to test"
  description="Six business decisions across three analyses: fare evasion sizing, a manufacturing control rule, two loyalty incentive questions, a tool ergonomics test, a workspace layout comparison and a severance pay dispute. Case study by Linh (Min) Le."
  project="Decision Sciences and Operations Analysis"
  period="Fall 2023"
  tint="ai"
  hook="An employee said her severance offer was too low. The analysis was commissioned to test that, and the honest answer was that the data did not back her. Getting to a finding a client does not want is the part of this work that transfers."
  metrics={metrics}
>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">The decision this informed</h2>
        <p>
          An employee at ABC Inc. was offered seven weeks of severance and believed that was below
          what comparably situated colleagues had received. She was prepared to say so formally, which
          put the company in a position where an informal answer would not do. The question handed to
          our team was narrow and had to stay narrow: given what the company pays people with her age,
          tenure and salary, is seven weeks anomalous.
        </p>
        <p>
          A question with legal exposure has a property most analysis does not. It has to survive
          challenge from whichever side the answer disappoints, which rules out choosing a model
          because it produced a convenient result.
        </p>
        <p>
          This was one of six business problems across the term, each from a different operating
          context and each ending in a written recommendation rather than a statistic. The other five
          are below.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">The finding</h2>
        <p>
          An employee at ABC Inc. was offered seven weeks of severance and believed that was below what
          comparable colleagues received. The question had legal weight, so the analysis had to be able
          to survive challenge in either direction.
        </p>
        <p>
          We built three competing regressions predicting weeks of severance from some combination of
          age, years at the company, pay and gender, then compared them on adjusted R-squared rather
          than raw R-squared, since raw R-squared rises whenever a predictor is added whether or not it
          earns its place.
        </p>
        <div class="cs-table-wrap">
          <table>
            <caption>Three candidate models on 50 employee records. The selected model is the one that keeps the most explanatory power with the fewest predictors.</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Model</th><th>Predictors</th><th>Adjusted R&sup2;</th><th>Contains 7 weeks</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th>A</th><td>Age, Years, Pay, Female</td><td>0.7316</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
              <tr><th>B</th><td>Years, Pay, Female, Pay &times; Female</td><td>0.7340</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
              <tr class="pick"><th>C, selected</th><td>Years, Pay, Female</td><td>0.7363</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Model C wins on the measure that matters and it is also the most defensible. Age and years at
          the company correlate at 0.81 in this dataset, which is unsurprising because older employees
          tend to have longer tenure, and carrying both means carrying the same information twice. Once
          years is in the model, age stops adding anything, so dropping it makes the model more stable
          without costing explanatory power. Model B goes the other way and adds an interaction term
          between pay and gender, which does not reach significance and buys a lower adjusted
          R-squared for the complexity.
        </p>
        <p>
          The answer was then straightforward and unwelcome. The interval within which a severance
          offer for someone with her age, tenure and pay would be expected to fall contains seven weeks
          under all three models, not just the selected one. Her offer sits inside the range the data
          predicts for her characteristics, so the analysis does not support the complaint. Reporting
          that clearly, to a client who commissioned the work, is the whole point of having run it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">The other decisions, and the limit each one had to state</h2>
        <p>
          The severance question is the one with stakes attached, but it is not the one that taught the
          most. Across all six problems the analytical work sat in the same two places every time.
          Choosing which instrument the question actually called for, since none of them arrived with a
          method attached. And then deciding how far the answer was allowed to reach, which is a
          judgement the statistics cannot make for you.
        </p>

        <h3 class="h-min">A manufacturer wanted to be told which rule to adopt</h3>
        <p>
          Fisher Inc. casts parts to a target length of 8.05 inches with a standard deviation of 0.180,
          measuring the mean of every batch of sixteen, and halts the line when a batch drifts too far.
          They were split between two sets of control limits and wanted a recommendation.
        </p>
        <p>
          A control limit is a hypothesis test wearing different clothes, so the two rules can be
          compared directly on the two ways they fail. A Type I error stops a line that was running
          fine. A Type II error lets a genuinely drifting process keep producing.
        </p>
        <Figure
          src="/figures/decision-control-rule-tradeoff.png"
          alt="Grouped bars comparing the two control rules on their two failure modes. The wide rule almost never halts a healthy line but misses 78.2% of real drifts. The tight rule halts unnecessarily 22.65% of the time and misses only 13.3%."
          caption="The two failure modes trade places between the rules. Choosing one is choosing which error you would rather make."
          wide
        />
        <p>
          The wide rule almost never stops the line without cause and misses more than three quarters
          of real problems. The tight rule catches nearly nine in ten and shuts the line down
          unnecessarily on close to a quarter of batches. There is no version of this where one rule is
          simply better, so the memo did not pretend there was. It handed the trade-off back quantified,
          with the question that actually decides it: which costs Fisher more, an hour of unnecessary
          downtime or a defective casting reaching a customer. That number is theirs, and they had never
          put it on paper.
        </p>

        <h3 class="h-min">A transit authority needed a number it could budget against</h3>
        <p>
          A sample of 500 riders on a route carrying a million people a year at $7.50 a ticket, of whom
          90 boarded without paying. A one-sample z-interval on that proportion gives 14.63% to 21.37%
          at 95% confidence, which converts to roughly $1.1M to $1.6M of annual revenue walking through
          the gates. A range is the right shape of answer for a budget decision, and the memo said
          plainly that it describes this route rather than the network.
        </p>

        <h3 class="h-min">A marketing executive asked two questions that needed two methods</h3>
        <p>
          The first was whether a free flight offer beat free insurance. A two-sample z-interval on the
          difference in enrollment rates returns 0.128% to 2.396%, and because the interval excludes
          zero the difference is real rather than noise. Free flight wins, though the whole interval
          sits under two and a half points, which is worth saying out loud before anyone plans around it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The second was whether one campaign would do for everyone. That is an independence question,
          not a difference question, so it takes a chi-square test rather than another interval.
          Enrollment against prior-year spending category returns p below 0.0001. Customers in different
          spending categories do not behave alike, which is what justifies segment-targeted messaging
          over a single uniform campaign, and it changes where the budget goes.
        </p>

        <h3 class="h-min">A tool design question that only the pairing could answer</h3>
        <p>
          Twenty-five right-handed students each used a right-threaded and a left-threaded instrument.
          Because every person used both, a paired t-test compares each person against themselves and
          removes the variation in individual dexterity that would otherwise drown the effect being
          measured. Right-handed threads were faster at p = 0.013, with the true mean advantage between
          7.10 and 21.94 seconds at 90% confidence.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then the boundary, which is the part that matters. This holds for right-handed operators,
          because they are the only people tested. A tool standard for a whole workforce would need the
          left-handed test nobody ran, and the memo said so rather than letting the result quietly
          generalise.
        </p>

        <h3 class="h-min">A shop floor choosing between three layouts</h3>
        <p>
          Thirty observations of flow time, ten per design. One-way ANOVA rather than three separate
          t-tests, because running the comparisons separately inflates the false positive rate across
          the set. Overall p = 0.0058, so at least one design differs, though ANOVA will not say which.
          Tukey HSD identifies the pairs while holding the error rate across all three comparisons:
          design 1 against 2 at p = 0.0145 and design 1 against 3 at p = 0.0118 are both real, and
          design 2 against 3 at p = 0.996 is not.
        </p>
        <p>
          Designs 2 and 3 both beat design 1, at means of 3.47 and 3.45 days against 4.18, and are
          statistically indistinguishable from each other. So the recommendation was to adopt either and
          to choose between them on cost or floor space, since the data gives no reason to prefer one.
          Saying that the analysis stops here, and the decision continues elsewhere, is a more useful
          answer than manufacturing a winner.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">Assumptions and limitations</h2>
        <p>
          Random sampling was not explicitly confirmed in any of the six datasets. Every memo states
          this and proceeds with the caveat attached rather than assuming it away, because the
          inference each method licenses depends on it. Where a specific condition could not be
          verified, the conclusion was scoped to match.
        </p>
        <p>
          The fare evasion figure describes one route and should not be read as a network total. The
          workspace comparison covers three designs that were tested, not the space of layouts that
          exist. The ergonomics result covers right-handed operators only. The severance analysis rests
          on 50 employee records from one company, and it establishes that a seven-week offer is inside
          the predicted range for those characteristics, which is a narrower claim than saying the
          process that produced it was fair.
        </p>
        <p>
          The control rule comparison assumes measurements within a batch of sixteen are independent
          and that batch size is under 10% of the population, neither of which the brief confirms.
          Temperature, humidity and raw material quality all move casting length, so the standard
          deviation the whole calculation runs on is itself a quantity worth monitoring rather than a
          constant.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</CaseStudy>
